# Group homomorphisms and isomorphisms, modeled by the kernel. A homomorphism is a map between groups that respects the
# operation: f(a . b) = f(a) . f(b). From that one law, two facts follow for any homomorphism, and the kernel proves them
# here for a concrete one: it sends the identity to the identity, and it sends inverses to inverses. The example is
# NEGATION on Z/3, the map sending each element to its additive inverse. In an abelian group negation respects addition,
# so it is a homomorphism, it fixes the identity, and being its own inverse it is a bijection, hence an ISOMORPHISM of Z/3
# with itself (an automorphism). This is the bottom of the whole hierarchy of structure-preserving maps: homomorphism,
# monomorphism (injective), epimorphism (surjective), isomorphism (bijective), automorphism (to itself).

# Z/3, the group it acts on.
form z3
  case e0
  case e1
  case e2

# TEMPLATE: one entry of a single-valued map on elements, `from` sent to `to`. Replaces every
# `case .. / send back / make ..` block with one line per entry.
tree map-element
  take from
  take to

  hook fuse
    case {from}
      send back
        make {to}

# The group operation, addition modulo three.
task op
  take a, like z3
  take b, like z3
  like z3
  fork case, read a
    case e0
      send back
        read b
    case e1
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-element, e0, e1
        fuse map-element, e1, e2
        fuse map-element, e2, e0
    case e2
      fork case, read b
        fuse map-element, e0, e2
        fuse map-element, e1, e0
        fuse map-element, e2, e1

# The homomorphism: negation, sending each element to its additive inverse.
task f
  take a, like z3
  like z3
  fork case, read a
    fuse map-element, e0, e0
    fuse map-element, e1, e2
    fuse map-element, e2, e1

# THE HOMOMORPHISM LAW: f respects the operation, f (op a b) = op (f a) (f b), over all nine pairs. Negation distributes
# over addition because the group is abelian. This single law is the definition of a homomorphism.
rule f-is-a-homomorphism
  seat a, like z3
  seat b, like z3
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call f
        call op
          read a
          read b
      call op
        call f
          read a
        call f
          read b
  fold a, b

# A HOMOMORPHISM PRESERVES THE IDENTITY: f e0 = e0. The neutral element maps to the neutral element.
rule f-preserves-identity
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call f
        make e0
      make e0
  calm hold

# f IS BIJECTIVE (its own inverse): f (f a) = a, over the three elements. An invertible homomorphism is an ISOMORPHISM, so
# negation is an automorphism of Z/3, a symmetry of the group with itself.
rule f-is-an-isomorphism
  seat a, like z3
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call f
        call f
          read a
      read a
  fold a

# A HOMOMORPHISM PRESERVES INVERSES: the inverse of a in Z/3 is f a (negation IS the inverse map), and f sends a to its
# inverse, so f respects inversion. Here a + f(a) = e0 for every a, the defining property of an inverse, proved for all.
rule f-sends-element-to-its-inverse
  seat a, like z3
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call op
        read a
        call f
          read a
      make e0
  fold a
